\ seq.4 - a new builtin, written in Forth: `seq FIRST LAST`.
\ Load it, then use it like any command:
\     forth 'S" forth-shell-examples/seq.4" INCLUDED'
\     seq 1 5 | while read n; do echo "line $n"; done
\ A builtin is a Forth word that reads its arguments from ARGC and ARGV@
\ (NUL-terminated strings) and sets LAST-STATUS; BUILTIN names it.

: SEQ-NUM ( c-addr --- n )   \ a decimal number, digits only
  0 SWAP BEGIN DUP C@ DUP 48 58 WITHIN WHILE 48 - ROT 10 * + SWAP 1+ REPEAT 2DROP ;

: DO-SEQ ( --- )
  ARGC @ 3 < IF S" usage: seq FIRST LAST" ERR-TYPE ERR-NL 2 LAST-STATUS ! EXIT THEN
  2 ARGV@ SEQ-NUM 1+  1 ARGV@ SEQ-NUM
  2DUP > IF ?DO I N>STR DUP CSTRLEN TYPE CR LOOP ELSE 2DROP THEN
  0 LAST-STATUS ! ;

' DO-SEQ S" seq" BUILTIN
